# Envelope sender rewriting (spec §11.18).
#
# Every message this machine sends leaves as `<local user>@{{ mail.myhostname }}`
# unless something says otherwise, and no relay is authorised for that domain —
# so SPF fails, the relay rejects, and a site's mail silently does not arrive.
# This map is what makes a site send as its own domain instead.
#
# Applied to the envelope only (`sender_canonical_classes = envelope_sender`).
# SPF is evaluated against the envelope, so that is the address that has to be
# authorised; the From: the recipient sees stays whatever the application wrote.
#
# Postfix tries the most specific key first — `user@domain`, then `user`, then
# `@domain` — so the per-site entries below win over the catch-all, whatever
# order they appear in.
#
# Held at the same 0600 as the credential beside it. It carries no secret, but
# it is every domain hosted on this machine in one file: the customer list.
# Nothing but Postfix reads it, and Postfix reads it as root.
{% for sender in mail.senders %}

# {{ sender.site }}
{{ sender.local_user }}	{{ sender.address }}
{{ sender.local_user }}@{{ mail.myhostname }}	{{ sender.address }}
{% endfor %}

# Everything else this machine emits — cron's MAILTO, a script that set no
# sender, root's own mail — leaves as the relay's configured address rather
# than as a local account name the relay has never heard of.
@{{ mail.myhostname }}	{{ mail.default_sender }}
